216 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Understanding Generative AI in Creative Practice\n\n\n\nI\047ve taken a course on generative AI before\054 and I also use AI tools fairly often at my job\054 so my understanding of it comes less from theory and more from actually working with it\056 At its core\054 generative AI is a system trained on huge amounts of data\054 images\054 text\054 whatever the case may be\054 that learns patterns well enough to produce new content that fits those patterns\056 It\047s not \042thinking\042 the way people do\054 but it\047s very good at picking up on style\054 structure\054 and repetition\056\nFrom my experience\054 I don\047t see generative AI as a threat to designers\056 It\047s more like a tool that speeds up the parts of the process that used to take the longest\056 It\047s great for quickly testing ideas\054 generating variations\054 and seeing options I wouldn\047t have thought of on my own\054 which is basically what happened while I was working on the three images for this assignment\056 I also got the chance to visit SAT \050Soci\351t\351 des Arts Technologiques\051 and see one of their locally\055run AI models in action\056 That was interesting because it made me think about how differently AI can be built and used depending on who\047s running it and why\056 Not every model has to be a massive closed system\056\nThe work that actually inspires me is \042Hello Vincent\054\042 an application developed by the startup Jumbo Mana for Mus\351e d\047Orsay\054 connected to their Van Gogh exhibition\056 It lets visitors have a real conversation with an AI version of Van Gogh\054 trained on around 900 of his letters\054 while he \042paints\042 Wheatfield with Crows in front of them\056 What I like about this project is how openly it\047s presented\056 It\047s clearly labeled as an AI experience\054 built with an actual art historian\054 and it\047s honest about being a tool for learning\054 not a replacement for the real paintings\056\nThat honesty matters to me because AI art gets treated unfairly a lot of the time\056 In another class\054 we talked about an experiment where someone posted an actual\054 unaltered Van Gogh painting online but labeled it as AI generated\054 and people immediately started criticizing it\054 not because it looked bad\054 but purely because of the label\056 That stuck with me\056 A lot of the backlash against AI art isn\047t really about quality\054 it\047s about bias against the label itself\056 \042Hello Vincent\042 feels like the opposite of that\072 it doesn\047t hide what it is\054 and it still finds a genuinely meaningful way to bring people closer to an artist\047s work\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 217 0 R
     /Pg 214 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

